A1. Here's a fellow who takes things lightly. ~ PICKPOCKET
A10. You need openers to get into this. ~ CLAM
A14. All wet. ~ ADRIP
A15. Preposition. ~ INTO
A16. What the Bremen did to Scotland Light. ~ HOVE
A17. Straight from the bottle. ~ NEAT
A18. A kernel from a sound nut. ~ IDEA
A19. Puts it away and forgets it. ~ SAVES
A20. One man top in Scotland. ~ TAM
A21. The new Scotch wheeling. ~ FREE
A22. They bank everything in one good sock. ~ MISERS
A23. The Man in the Brown Derby. ~ AL
A24. With or without stocks and bonds you're this these days. ~ POOR
A25. Don't poke your nose into these. ~ FISTS
A26. There's a crib in this, for young calves of course. ~ BARN
A27. This kind of hood covers tracks. ~ FALSE
A28. Numb Prunes. ~ NP
A30. Svelte like Paul Whiteman. ~ OBESE
A32. A place for a dark man with a dark purpose. ~ ALLEY
A33. To converse deeply on a honeymoon. ~ COO
A34. This calls for deep penetration (pl.). ~ ORES
A35. Proverbial descendant of man (pl.). ~ EVILS
A36. Much pither, pother, dither and dother. ~ FUSS
A37. This makes "No" more forceful. ~ NOR
A38. These should be executed on time. ~ PLANS
A39. A sure sign something's missing. ~ CARET
A40. The way of a gentle zephyr. ~ SW
A41. The measure of a fisherman (two words). ~ SOBIG
A42. The gentler sex, in Brooklyn. ~ DAME
A43. Woman and song. ~ CAROL
A44. A tree worshipper (slang). ~ PEKE
A45. This means business, at least it used to in B.D. (Before Depression). ~ CO
A46. This is a lot of rot. ~ MILDEW
A48. Finis! ~ DONE
A49. The way a broadened man travels. ~ FAR
A50. Big eaters. ~ ACIDS
A51. The man to be sure to bring to the altar. ~ BEST
A52. What shysters make you do with your money. ~ PART
A53. It's just the same in France. ~ MEME
A54. The kind of story told at 4 a.m. ~ LAME
A55. Delegates will be at this next month, despite the nut factories. ~ LARGE
A56. This port isn't to be drunk. ~ ADEN
A57. A June bug. ~ BRIDEGROOM

D1. These hung around your great grandfather and he didn't dare leave home without them. ~ PANTALOONS
D2. Philosopher's pipe dream. ~ IDEAL
D3. To chock nuts full. ~ CRAM
D4. A trunk for your troubles. ~ KIT
D5. Put on the soft pedal when you see this. ~ PP
D6. It may be hard but you'll get a big kick out of it. ~ CIDER
D7. A bouncer at a lowdown joint. ~ KNEE
D8. A bitta pi usually follows this. ~ ETA
D9. This precedes "love, honor and obey." ~ TO
D10. What every young girl should be. ~ CHASTE
D11. His Old Numbers. ~ LOVES
D12. Swear roundly. ~ AVER
D13. Naval engagement at noon. ~ MESS
D18. A will you can't break. ~ IRON
D19. Just like a woman. ~ SISSY
D21. This will put you in fair way to swing. ~ FORE
D22. It takes a lot of feet to cover these. ~ MILES
D24. What to do with a yarborough. ~ PASS
D25. Comedians take plenty of these. ~ FALLS
D26. A few hops with a stick. ~ BEER
D27. What Scotch blades have for a shilling. ~ FLING
D28. Bugle. ~ NOSE
D29. The fight at the finish: and the revival of a play. ~ POSTMORTEM
D31. A constant knitter. ~ BROW
D32. To take advantage of (and it's not what you're thinking) ~ AVAIL
D33. A successful pickup. ~ CURE
D35. A good bender. ~ ELBOW
D36. Rep. ~ FAME
D38. Little breathers. ~ PORES
D39. The kind of walk they take in Harlem. ~ CAKE
D41. To create the mood indigo. ~ SADDEN
D42. A small depression. ~ DENT
D43. This goes for all kinds of weather. ~ CLIME
D44. Held an artistic position. ~ POSED
D45. This requires a strong hold. ~ CARGO
D46. The cry of youth. ~ MAMA
D47. Chilled. ~ ICED
D48. A John with a small neck. ~ DEMI
D49. Here's a gamble for you. ~ FARO
D51. A lean to. ~ BAR
D52. The way to do justice to any course. ~ PAR
